    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-l/q3-question-1.svg\" alt=\"Find the solutions of the trigonometric equation 6 13sin 2 + ( θ + α ) ° = 5cos2 θ °, 0 ≤ θ &lt; 360 5 where tan α° = , 0 &lt; α &lt; 90 . ( ) 7 12\" style=\"--madas-width:584.0px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-l/q3-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"The acute angle has sin(α◦) = 5/13 [B1] and cos(α◦) = 12/13. [A1] Start with 6 + 13 sin(2θ + α) = 5 cos 2θ, with angles in degrees: The addition formula gives 6 + 13(sin 2θ cos α + cos 2θ sin α) = 5 cos 2θ.\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-l/q3-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"Substitution of the sides of the 5–12–13 triangle gives 6 + 12 sin 2θ + 5 cos 2θ = 5 cos 2θ. Expand the sine and cancel the two 5 cos((2θ)◦) terms: This gives sin((2θ)◦) = −1/2. [A1] The principal inverse sine is −30◦, so 2θ = −30◦+ 360◦n or 210◦+ 360◦n. Hence θ = −15◦+ 180◦n or 105◦+ 180◦n. In 0 ≤2θ &lt; 720, [A1] the values of 2θ are 210, 330, 570, 690. [A1] Thus θ = 105, 165, 285, 345 [A1] in degrees: [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 3 is 7 marks.</p>"
    ],
