      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-l/q4-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"(a) y′ = cos x −x sin x. [B1] [A1] Indeed y = x cos x, so dy/dx = 1 · cos x + x(−sin x). At a stationary point 0 = cos x −x sin x; thus cos x = x sin x. Divide both sides by x cos x. At the interior stationary point, tan x = 1/x, [A1] giving x = arctan(1/x) [A1] on 0 &lt; x &lt; π/2 . [A1] (b) Using the source convention, f(x) = x −arctan(1/x) gives f(0.8) = −0.096055 . . . &lt; 0, f(1) = 0.214601 . . . &gt; 0. The opposite sign convention gives the same root. The continuous function g(x) = arctan(1/x) −x satisfies g(0.8) ≈0.0961 &gt; 0 [B1] and g(1) = π/4 −1 &lt; 0.\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-l/q4-solution-3.svg\" alt=\"Hence α lies between 0.8 and 1. [A1] (c) Apply xn+1 = arctan(1/xn) in radians, using the unrounded result as the next input: Iterating from 0.9 gives x2 ≈0.838 , x3 ≈0.873 , and x4 ≈0.853 . [A1] (d) Start at x1. Each vertical step to y = arctan(1/x) [B1] gives the next ordinate, and each horizontal step to y = x converts it to the next abscissa: [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 4 is 10 marks.</p>"
