  {
    "id": "madas/alevel-pure-l/q8",
    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-l/q8-question-1.svg\" alt=\"The first three terms of a geometric series are u = q ( 4 p + 1 ) , u = q ( 2 p + 3 ) and u = q ( 2 p − 3 ) . 1 2 3 a) Find the possible values of p . ( ) 7 The sum to infinity of the series is 250 . b) Find the value of q . ( ) 4\" style=\"--madas-width:585.3px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-l/q8-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"(a) The successive terms obey q(4p + 1)r = q(2p + 3) and q(2p + 3)r = q(2p −3). Eliminating r by dividing gives (4p + 1)/(2p + 3) = (2p + 3)/(2p −3). The square of the middle term equals the product of the others: [B1] [A1] Divide by q2, then expand to 8p2 −10p −3 = 4p2 + 12p + 9.\" style=\"--madas-width:678.7px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-l/q8-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"Collecting terms gives 4p2 −22p −12 = 0; division by 2 and factorisation give (2p + 3)2 = (4p + 1)(2p −3), 2p2 −11p −6 = (2p + 1)(p −6) = 0. [M1] [M1] [A1] Thus p = −1 2 or 6 . [M1] [A1] (b) At p = −1 2 the first three terms are −q, 2q, −4q, so the common ratio is −2, which is divergent: [M1] At p = 6, the terms are 25q, 15q, 9q. Only this case has |r| &lt; 1. Its first term is 25q and the ratio 3/5. [A1] Thus 250 = 25q/(1 −3/5) [A1] hence 250 = 25q/(2/5), 100 = 25q, and q = 4 . [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 8 is 11 marks.</p>"
    ],
    "promptText": "The first three terms of a geometric series are u = q ( 4 p + 1 ) , u = q ( 2 p + 3 ) and u = q ( 2 p − 3 ) . 1 2 3 a) Find the possible values of p . ( ) 7 The sum to infinity of the series is 250 . b) Find the value of q . ( ) 4"
  },
